        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n73",
        "text": "[English: complete approved edition prose]\nReturn to the experiment from the previous lesson, which examined refraction at air-water and air-glass interfaces.\nThis time, consider only light traveling from an optically denser medium into an optically less dense one, such as from water or glass into air. Optical density here refers to a higher or lower refractive index, not to mass density.\n\nIn the apparatus containing the glass semicylinder, place the light source in the lower half of the disk.\nDirect the incident light along the radius previously followed by the refracted light. It leaves the glass along the former incident direction: the incident and refracted paths exchange roles, illustrating the reversibility of the light path.\n\nInside the glass, there is reflected light as well as incident light.\nThe trace of the reflected light is visible on the frosted surface of the semicylinder (Figure 156).\nReflection still follows the law of reflection: the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.\n\nIf $n$ is the refractive index of glass relative to air, with $n>1$, then the refractive index of air relative to glass is $\\frac{1}{n}$.\nIn this experiment, glass is the first medium and air is the second.\nWhen a propagating refracted ray exists and the angle of incidence is nonzero, the law of refraction can be written in the following sine-ratio form.\n\nFigure 156\n\nFigure 156\n\n$\\frac{\\sin\\alpha}{\\sin\\beta}=\\frac{1}{n}$ (6.12)\n\nThe angle in the numerator is the angle of incidence; the angle in the denominator is the angle of refraction. Both are measured from the normal to the interface. At normal incidence both angles are zero, so use the law of refraction without division rather than directly evaluating this sine ratio.\n\nNow move the light source to gradually increase the angle of incidence.\nWhile a propagating refracted ray still exists, both the reflection angle, which equals the incidence angle, and the refraction angle increase.\nFor a nonzero angle of incidence, the refraction angle is greater than the incidence angle.\n\nEquation (6.12) gives $\\sin\\beta=n\\sin\\alpha$.\nSince $n>1$ here and the incidence angle is nonzero, $\\sin\\beta>\\sin\\alpha$.\nFor incidence and refraction angles between zero and a right angle, sine increases with the angle; therefore $\\beta>\\alpha$.\n\nThus, when light passes from an optically denser medium into an optically less dense one, the refracted ray bends away from the normal, provided that it still propagates and incidence is not normal.\n\nAs the incidence angle increases toward the critical condition in this experiment, the reflected beam becomes brighter and carries a larger share of the light energy, while the refracted beam becomes dimmer and carries a smaller share. This describes the experimental observation; visual brightness is not itself a direct measurement of light energy.\nAs the refraction angle approaches $90^\\circ$, the energy carried by the refracted beam falls particularly rapidly.\nWhen the refracted beam travels almost along the interface (Figure 157), the reflected share of the energy approaches $100\\%$. This limit is understood in the ideal model of a transparent interface without absorption.\n\nThe incidence angle $\\alpha_0$ corresponds to the limiting refraction angle $\\beta=$ $90^\\circ$. At this boundary, the refracted direction lies along the interface: it is a grazing direction.\nContinue rotating the light source until the incidence angle $\\alpha$ is strictly greater than $\\alpha_0$. There is then no refracted beam propagating into the second medium; in the ideal model just described, all the light energy is reflected back into the first medium. This is **total internal reflection**.\n\nFigure 157\n\nFigure 157\n\nThe source describes total internal reflection at an optically denser-to-less-dense interface using an incidence angle $\\alpha$ greater than or equal to $\\alpha_0$. To distinguish the boundary precisely, equality corresponds to a grazing refracted direction along the interface; only an angle strictly greater than the critical angle eliminates the refracted beam propagating into the less dense medium.\nThe incidence angle $\\alpha_0$ corresponding to a refraction angle of $90^\\circ$ is called the **critical angle for total internal reflection**.\nWhen $\\sin\\beta=1$, equation (6.12) becomes:\n\n$\\sin\\alpha_0=\\frac{1}{n}$ (6.13)\n\nThis equation can be used to find the critical angle $\\alpha_0$.\n\nThe source gives the following approximate example for water: $n=1.33$, with a critical angle of about $48^\\circ35^\\prime$.\nThe source's approximate examples for glass are $n=1.5$ and a critical angle of about $41^\\circ50^\\prime$; for diamond they are $n=2.4$ and about $24^\\circ40^\\prime$.\nAir is the second medium in all these examples. The listed indices and angles are retained as the source's approximate, rounded examples, not as exact constants.\n\nFigure 158 shows beams emitted by a light source a short distance below the water surface.\nThicker ray lines represent stronger light.\n\nFigure 158\n\nFigure 158\n\nA simple experiment also lets you observe total internal reflection.\nFill a glass with water and raise it slightly above eye level.\nLook at the water surface from below through the side of the glass. In a viewing direction where total internal reflection occurs, reflected light makes the surface look bright and silvery.\n\nFiber optics uses total internal reflection to carry light and images along bundles of transparent, flexible fibers. Such an arrangement is a light guide.\nA glass light guide consists of cylindrical glass fibers. In the usual core-and-cladding structure, each fiber has transparent cladding with a refractive index lower than that of its glass core.\nRepeated total internal reflections let light follow a straight or curved fiber. Bends must still preserve the interface and incidence-angle conditions needed for guidance; an arbitrarily sharp bend need not keep the light in the fiber.\n\nMany such fiber bundles can be gathered into a ribbon-like light guide.\nWhen an image is transmitted, each fiber carries the light corresponding to one image element (Figure 159). To reproduce the image at the output, the relative arrangement of the fibers must correspond at both ends.\nThese fiber-bundle light guides can be used in medicine to observe and examine internal organs. Sending light through a guide avoids the unwanted local heating from placing an ordinary light bulb directly at the observation site.\n\nFigure 159\n\nFigure 159"
      },
